{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786429287,"nonce":"Oy0u5HjeinCrWVDsNDvFfNlJun8=","title":"B17 chunking: the code, its own comment and the SPEC disagree","message":"**What is wrong.** `applyChunks` carries this comment:\n\n\u003e A batch at or under ChunkThreshold is ONE transaction, which is the common case and the fast one.\n\nIt is not. The loop slices by `ChunkSize` (500) **unconditionally**, so a 2 000-alert batch runs in **four** transactions of 500, not one. `ChunkThreshold` (2 000) gates only whether the batch is marked `partial` while the chunks run.\n\nSPEC §L.3.2's B17 row reads the same way the comment does ('500 per transaction, batches \u003e 2000 are split'), so all three statements of the rule disagree with each other.\n\n**Why it matters.** B17 is a durability and latency bound on the ingest hot path, and it is the one an operator reasons about when a storm arrives. Someone tuning `ChunkThreshold` expecting it to control transaction count will change nothing; someone reading the SPEC will predict the wrong number of transactions and the wrong `partial` window. Two of the three statements are wrong and nothing forced them to agree.\n\n**Where.** `internal/ingestion/decode/bounds.go` (`applyChunks` and its doc comment), `internal/ingestion/domain/bounds.go` (`ChunkSize`, `ChunkThreshold`), `docs/design/SPEC.md` §L.3.2 row B17.\n\n**Done when.** The three agree — either the code short-circuits to one transaction at or under the threshold as the comment promises, or the comment and the SPEC are corrected to say that chunking is unconditional and the threshold governs only `partial` marking. Current behaviour is pinned by `internal/ingestion/decode/bounds_boundary_test.go`, which asserts the real chunk sequences and says in its doc comment which sentence is wrong.","files":null}]}